solutions.latex:
From part (a), $g(x)=-\sin 2x$, with amplitude $1$ and period $\dfrac{2\pi}{2}=\pi$, so there are two full periods on $[0,2\pi]$.

The graph is an upside-down sine curve: it crosses zero at $x=0,\dfrac{\pi}{2},\pi,\dfrac{3\pi}{2},2\pi$, with minima of $-1$ at $x=\dfrac{\pi}{4},\dfrac{5\pi}{4}$ and maxima of $1$ at $x=\dfrac{3\pi}{4},\dfrac{7\pi}{4}$.

\exfigure{1593_q1(e)_s1}

Intercepts: $f(x)=x^3(4-x)=0$ gives $x=0$ or $x=4$, so the $x$-intercepts are $(0,0)$ and $(4,0)$. The $y$-intercept is $(0,0)$.

There is also a change in concavity where $f''(x)=12x(2-x)=0$, at $x=0$ and $x=2$. Since $f(2)=32-16=16$, there is a point of inflection at $(2,16)$. As $x\to\pm\infty$, $f(x)\to-\infty$.

\exfigure{1593_q2(b)_s1}

